Raising a matrix to a high power like 27, 31, or 40 seems daunting, but maybe there is a pattern or a shortcut that can help us. One thing I notice is that $\mathbf{A}$ has a simple relationship with its inverse: $\mathbf{A}^{-1} = -\mathbf{A}.$ This is because $\mathbf{A}^2 = -\mathbf{I},$ where $\mathbf{I}$ is the identity matrix. This means that any odd power of $\mathbf{A}$ is either equal to $\mathbf{A}$ or $-\mathbf{A}$, depending on whether the exponent is divisible by 4 or not. For example, $\mathbf{A}^3 = -\mathbf{A}^2\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{A},$ and $\mathbf{A}^5 = -\mathbf{A}^4\mathbf{A} = -\mathbf{A}.$ This makes it much easier to compute $\mathbf{A}^{27} + \mathbf{A}^{31} + \mathbf{A}^{40},$ since we only need to know the sign of each term. We can use modular arithmetic to find the remainder of dividing each exponent by 4. For 27, the remainder is 3, so $\mathbf{A}^{27} = \mathbf{A}.$ For 31, the remainder is 3 as well, so $\mathbf{A}^{31} = \mathbf{A}.$ For 40, the remainder is 0, so $\mathbf{A}^{40} = \mathbf{I}.$ Therefore, $\mathbf{A}^{27} + \mathbf{A}^{31} + \mathbf{A}^{40} = \mathbf{A} + \mathbf{A} + \mathbf{I} = 2\mathbf{A} + \mathbf{I}.$ To find the actual matrix, we just need to add 2 times the entries of $\mathbf{A}$ to the entries of $\mathbf{I}$: 
\[\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}^{27} + \mathbf{A}^{31} + \mathbf{A}^{40} &= 2\mathbf{A} + \mathbf{I} \\
&= 2\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 7 \\ -1 & -2 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 6 & 14 \\ -2 & -4 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 7 & 14 \\ -2 & -3 \end{pmatrix}
\end{aligned}\]

# Answer

\begin{pmatrix} 7 & 14 \\ -2 & -3 \end{pmatrix}

