question:
Expand and simplify completely: \begin{align*}
x\left(x(1+x)+2x\right)-3(x^2-x+2)
\end{align*}

solution_with_answer:
To expand and simplify completely, I need to distribute the x's in the parentheses and then combine like terms. I'll start with the left side of the expression: \begin{align*}
x\left(x(1+x)+2x\right) &= x(x^2+x+2x) \quad \text{(distribute x)}\\
&= x(x^2+3x) \quad \text{(combine like terms)}\\
&= x^3+3x^2 \quad \text{(distribute x again)}
\end{align*} Now I'll do the same for the right side of the expression: \begin{align*}
-3(x^2-x+2) &= -3x^2+3x-6 \quad \text{(distribute -3)}
\end{align*} To finish, I need to subtract the right side from the left side, which means changing the signs of the terms on the right side: \begin{align*}
x\left(x(1+x)+2x\right)-3(x^2-x+2) &= x^3+3x^2-(-3x^2+3x-6)\\
&= x^3+3x^2+3x^2-3x+6 \quad \text{(change signs)}\\
&= x^3+6x^2-3x+6 \quad \text{(combine like terms)}
\end{align*}

# Answer

x^3+6x^2-3x+6

